{
  "esm": "0.8.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "latlon3d_transport_cwc_3d",
    "description": "Three-dimensional consistency with continuity (CWC) on the latlon3d grid: the free-stream-preservation gate for the whole horizontal+vertical flux-form transport wave. Carries the AIR MASS (m, a pressure thickness) alongside the TRACER MASS (mq) and derives the mixing ratio q = mq/m as an observed, so that six rules fire in one model -- three bare-D face-flux-divergence rules closing continuity (lon periodic, lat polar, lev no-flux) and three compound flux-form advection rules carrying the tracer (D(Mx*q,lon), D(My*q,lat), D(Mz*q,lev)). The three winds are shape-disjoint (Mx over [lon_nodes,lat,lev], My over [lon,lat_nodes,lev], Mz over [lon,lat,lev_nodes]) and q is over [lon,lat,lev], so esm-spec 9.6.1 shape constraints -- which compare index-set NAMES, not sizes -- select each rule unambiguously with no priority games. THE PROPERTY UNDER TEST: at q == 1 the donor flux collapses to F = 0.5*(M*(1+1) - |M|*(1-1)) = M with every IEEE operation exact, so each compound divergence becomes BITWISE identical to its bare-D twin; both right-hand sides are spelled with the same summation order, so dm/dt and dmq/dt are bitwise equal and mq tracks m to the last bit forever, holding q == 1 EXACTLY (not approximately) while the divergent wind relocates a large fraction of the air mass. This single assertion exercises the rule-enforced zonal wrap, the structural polar-face omission, the cos(phi)/sin-difference area weights and the vertical wall closure SIMULTANEOUSLY: if any pair of operators disagrees about a face, a weight or a wall, the cancellation is no longer bitwise. Mz is a POLYNOMIAL in pe (4z(1-z)(1-2z)) so it vanishes EXACTLY at both walls in IEEE -- a trig expression that merely vanishes analytically does not (sin(2*pi) is -2.45e-16) -- and it reverses sign mid-column so both donor branches are exercised. The mixing-ratio form of the same transport is the NEGATIVE CONTROL: driven by this same divergent wind it does NOT preserve a constant tracer, which is the gap that carrying the air mass repairs.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:problem",
      "grid:latlon3d",
      "family:finite_volume",
      "op:D",
      "axes:lon,lat,lev",
      "scheme:flux_form",
      "property:free_stream"
    ]
  },
  "models": {
    "TransportCWC3d": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "../grids/latlon3d/rules/face_flux_divergence_lon_periodic.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": 12,
            "NLAT": 7,
            "NLEV": 6
          }
        },
        {
          "ref": "../grids/latlon3d/rules/face_flux_divergence_lat_polar.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": 12,
            "NLAT": 7,
            "NLEV": 6
          }
        },
        {
          "ref": "../grids/latlon3d/rules/face_flux_divergence_lev_noflux_bc.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": 12,
            "NLAT": 7,
            "NLEV": 6
          }
        },
        {
          "ref": "../grids/latlon3d/rules/upwind1_flux_D_lon_periodic.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": 12,
            "NLAT": 7,
            "NLEV": 6
          }
        },
        {
          "ref": "../grids/latlon3d/rules/upwind1_flux_D_lat_polar.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": 12,
            "NLAT": 7,
            "NLEV": 6
          }
        },
        {
          "ref": "../grids/latlon3d/rules/upwind1_flux_D_lev_noflux_bc.esm",
          "bindings": {
            "NLON": 12,
            "NLAT": 7,
            "NLEV": 6
          }
        }
      ],
      "variables": {
        "m": {
          "type": "state",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Air mass per cell (a pressure thickness). Strictly positive."
        },
        "mq": {
          "type": "state",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Tracer mass per cell. Initialised bitwise equal to m, so q == 1."
        },
        "dev": {
          "type": "state",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lon",
            "lat",
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Diagnostic deviation dev = mq - m, obtained by integrating the DIFFERENCE of the two right-hand sides from dev(0) = 0. This is the quantity the conformance assertion gates: at q == 1 the tracer divergence collapses bitwise onto the continuity divergence, so dev's own right-hand side is EXACTLY 0.0 in IEEE and dev never leaves zero. It exists because assertion `reference` expressions are build-time and cellwise and cannot read states or observeds, so q itself is not assertable and asserting mq would require re-spelling the entire discrete divergence by hand -- i.e. duplicating the operator inside its own test. Against a constant-zero reference there is nothing for the test to get wrong. dev feeds nothing back into m or mq."
        },
        "lon0_deg": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "deg",
          "default": 0.0,
          "description": "Grid's lon0_deg free name; 0 closes the periodic zonal circle."
        },
        "lat0_deg": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "deg",
          "default": -90.0,
          "description": "Grid's lat0_deg free name; -90 for the pole-to-pole global recipe."
        },
        "dlon_deg": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "deg",
          "expression": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              360.0,
              12
            ]
          },
          "description": "Zonal spacing 360/NLON = 30 deg, closing the periodic zonal circle. Spelled as a LITERAL because NLON is pinned at the expression_template_imports edge and is therefore consumed there -- a metaparameter bound at an import edge is not a live name in the importing document's scope (esm-spec 9.7.6). This case is a free-stream IDENTITY gate, not a resolution study: the property under test is bitwise exactness, which is resolution-independent, so a single small mesh is the right shape for it. The identity was separately verified at (NLON,NLAT,NLEV) = (8,5,6), (16,9,12) and (24,13,18) by driving the evaluator directly."
        },
        "dlat_deg": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "deg",
          "expression": {
            "op": "/",
            "args": [
              180.0,
              6
            ]
          },
          "description": "Meridional spacing 180/(NLAT-1) = 30 deg, pole-to-pole endpoint-inclusive; NLAT = 7 gives two half-polar caps plus five interior rows, so the cap stencils and the interior stencil are exercised separately. Spelled as a literal for the same import-edge reason as dlon_deg."
        },
        "pe": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "Vertical edge coordinate, uniform on [0,1], monotone increasing with lev (k=1 is the SURFACE).",
          "expression": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "output_idx": [
              "kn"
            ],
            "args": [],
            "ranges": {
              "kn": {
                "from": "lev_nodes"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "/",
              "args": [
                {
                  "op": "-",
                  "args": [
                    "kn",
                    1
                  ]
                },
                6
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        "dz": {
          "type": "observed",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Layer thicknesses from the grid's lev_thickness template.",
          "expression": {
